title: One-Shot Timers
files:
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex09_OneShot2P.em.ts
  - em.core:em.hal/OneShotI.em.ts
  - rpi.2040:rpi.distro.2040/BoardC.em.ts
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex08_OneShot1P.em.ts
  - .assets/img-101-04-01.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - open 4
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;%[cu]OneShotP% &ndash; Timer Events}

      Once again, we'll blink `AppLed` &ndash; but now we'll use a {[cu]OneShot} {[cs]$delegate} to sleep for a given period of time.

      This tour actually covers two example programs:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cu]OneShot1P}, which uses a primitive event loop<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} {[cu]OneShot2P}, which relies upon fiber dispatch

      Because of its similarity to the {[cu]FiberP} program viewed in the prior tour, we'll begin with {[cu]OneShot2P}.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 A 9
      - mark 1 B 34
      - mark 1 C 39
    focus: [1, 8]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]OneShot2P% &ndash; Details}

      Like our earlier {[cu]FiberP} example, {[cu]OneShot2P} defines a {[cf]blinkFB} callback dispatched by {[cu]FiberMgr}.

      {[BM,A]} The {[cu]OneShot} {[cs]$delegate} exported by {[cu]BoardC} im&shy;plements the {[cu]OneShotI} **Interface** (coming next).

      {[BM,B]} {[cf]uenable} arms an underlying HW timer to sleep for *n* &mu;s and trigger a {[cn]handler} callback upon awakening.

      {[BM,C]} The {[cf]handler} function itself then calls {[cf]post}, which in turn schedules the next cycle of {[cf]blinkFB}.

      </div>

      In this example, {[cu]FiberMgr} detects an *empty* internal {[cn]ready_list} after our {[cf]blinkFB} fiber body returns.

      Recalling its implementation, {[cu]FiberMgr} then enters a HW sleep mode via the {[cu]Common}.{[cn]Idle} proxy.

  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - mark 2 A 2
      - mark 2 B 4
      - mark 2 C 6
    focus: [2, 1]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]OneShotI% &ndash; Details}

      While you may have browsed to other `em.hal` inter&shy;faces, let's use {[cu]OneShotI} to explain this **EMS** pattern.

      {[BM,A]} Similar to boilerplate found in every **EMS Unit**, we here {[cs]$declare} the "flavor" of {[cu]OneShotI}.

      {[BM,B]} In general, an **EMS Interface** can define *constants* and *types* which support its published methods.

      {[BM,C]} A **_TypeScript_** interface conventionally named {[cs]$I} specifies the signature of every client function.

      </div>

      In contrast to a **Module** (which has a _concrete_ imple&shy;mentation), an **EMS Interface** must remain _abstract_.

      {[in]<b>Interfaces</b> can't expose %[cs]$config% or %[cs]$proxy% objects &ndash; only abstract methods which suppliers implement.}

  - cmds:
      - open 3
      - mark 3 I 18
      - mark 3 E 24
    focus: [3, 1]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

      {[ht,$peek]&thinsp;%[cu]BoardC% &ndash; A Quick Look}

      The {[cs]'@$distro/BoardC.em'} **Composite** imported by client programs will define the {[cu]OneShot} binding.

      {[cu]BoardC} imports {[bm,I]} a board-specific implementation of the abstract {[cu]OneShotI} **Interface**.

      Note how {[cu]BoardC} uses {[cu]OneShot} as an _alias_ which refer&shy;ences a **Module** source file of a different name.

      {[in]Click on the %[cu]OneShot% identifer and press <b>F12</b> to open the actual implementation file for &thinsp;<span class="em codicon codicon-circuit-board"></span>&thinsp; <span class="em">Board</span>&thinsp;.}

      {[cu]BoardC} will then explicitly {[ck]export} {[cu]OneShot}{[bm,E]},&thinsp; making its binding visible to clients of this **Composite**.

  - cmds:
      - mark 1 C 38
      - mark 1 D 29
    focus: [1, 1]
    acts: [$build]
    text: |

      {[ht,$build]&thinsp;%[cu]OneShot2P% &ndash; Build&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;Load}

      Click the {[cb,build]} action button to execute the {[cu]OneShot2P} program on our target {[em]%[ci,circuit-board]%&nbsp;Board}&thinsp;.

      While similar to other programs we've seen exe&shy;cute, the {[cu]OneShot2P} signal capture paints a different picture.

      Triggered by an underlying timer interrupt, {[cf]handler} uses `DbgC` {[bm,C]} to mark this point of awakening.

      Dispatched by {[cu]FiberMgr} (after _re-enabling_ interrupts), the {[cf]blinkFB} fiber body marks its entry using `DbgD` {[bm,D]}.

      The time between {[bm,C]} and {[bm,D]} corresponds to our pro&shy;gram's _interrupt latency_ &ndash; which we'll measure next.

  - cmds:
      - view 5
    text: |

      {[ht,monitor_heart]&thinsp;&thinsp;%[cu]OneShot2P% &ndash; Signal Capture}

      More activity on the `Dbg` liness&thinsp;&hellip;&thinsp;Recall that these sig&shy;nals assert active-low &ndash; {[cd]%%b+} would drive `DbgB` to 0.

      By convention, {[cu]Common}.{[cn]Idle} delegates use `DbgB` to delineate the MCU's current active&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;sleep mode.

      `DbgB` at marker {[dc,A]} reflects an inactive MCU awaiting a timer interrupt &ndash; the dominant state of {[cu]OneShot2P}.

      At marker {[dc,B]} `DbgB` now refects an active MCU &ndash; busy-waiting for 5&thinsp;ms after turning on `AppLed`.

      Looking closer, we measure 5&thinsp;&mu;s between the {[cd]%%c} at label {[dc,C]} and {[cd]%%d} at {[dc,D]} &ndash; our wakeup overhead.

  - cmds:
      - mark 4 A 10
      - mark 4 B 13
      - mark 4 C 21
      - mark 4 D 27
    focus: [4, 1]
    acts: [$reveal]
    text: |

      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]OneShot1P% &ndash; Details}

      The {[cu]OneShot1P} program behaves identically to our {[cu]OneShot2P} program &ndash; but without using the {[cu]FiberMgr}.

      Instead, {[cu]OneShot1P} uses a _super-loop_ design &ndash; which relies on app-specific flags to gate program execution.

      Here, {[cu]OneShot1P} uses its (volatile) {[cn]done_flag}{[bm,A]} to synchronize {[cf]handler} with the super-loop.

      After clearing {[cn]done_flag}, {[cs]em$run} awaits {[bm,C]} a signal from the timer {[cf]handler}{[bm,D]} before the loop continues.

      {[cu]OneShot1P} explicitly manages {[cn]GlobalInterrupts}{[bm,B]} &ndash; a need otherwise managed implicitly by {[cu]FiberMgr}.

      {[in] Prefer %[cu]FiberMgr% &ndash; but we will visit this pattern again. }

  - cmds:
      - open 4
    focus: [4, 1]
    acts: [$build]
    text: |

      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Execute {[cu]OneShotP} {[cb,build]} and view its signal capture.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Measure the {[cd]%%c}&rarr;{[cd]%%d} wakeup overhead.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Quantify (and rationalize) the cost of {[cu]FiberMgr}.

  - cmds:
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |

      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      The next tour examines use of the {[cu]OneShotI} **Interface** within a portable utility **Module**.

      We'll also have more to say about scheduling the MCU when actively executing.

      {[hc]}
